title wholesale rename: a pool whose every name changes at once

# Built to test emission pairing; it tests REALIZATION instead, and
# that is why it is kept.
#
# Off-of renames every name of three pools. Onto is identical to
# base, so nothing conflicts. The decision materializes all three
# rather than reusing their onto dnodes: a move of two names to two
# names has no unambiguous successor, so succession records nothing;
# with no substitution each output name looks itself up and finds
# nothing in onto; with no contributor, Section 12 creates the pool
# fresh. Seven unlinks and three materializations where no material
# changed.
#
# WHERE THE PROBLEM IS NOT. An earlier version of this comment said
# a plan preserving identity exists -- link the new names off an old
# one, then unlink -- and that is a workaround for a problem the
# plan does not have. At the filesystem level "mv x aa; mv y bb"
# keeps the pool trivially: after the first move it holds {aa, y},
# the same dnode, and the link count never approaches zero.
#
# WHERE IT IS. Realization asks a POOL-level question -- which onto
# dnode does this output pool reuse -- through a NAME-level
# mechanism: substitute a name, ask who holds it in onto. Names are
# exactly what goes blank when succession is ambiguous, which is
# open problem 6. Meanwhile the pool-level answer is sitting in the
# model the whole time: base, onto and off-of all carry (5, 100), so
# red edges put the three in ONE COMPONENT, and components span all
# three trees by construction.
#
# So the cost of OP6's caution is not only an unnamed successor. It
# is the pool losing its dnode -- data copied rather than relinked,
# and any external inode reference broken -- because a fact the
# component already holds is being derived through names instead.
# That is a decide-side question and is with the lead and the
# researcher.
#
# Pinned here so the behaviour is recorded whichever way it settles.

tree base
  1-1: /
    5-100: x = xv0
    5-100: y
    6-100: m = mv0
    6-100: n
    7-100: p = pv0
    7-100: q
    7-100: r

tree onto
  1-1: /
    5-100: x = xv0
    5-100: y
    6-100: m = mv0
    6-100: n
    7-100: p = pv0
    7-100: q
    7-100: r

tree offof
  1-1: /
    5-100: aa = xv0
    5-100: bb
    6-100: cc = mv0
    6-100: dd
    6-100: ee
    7-100: ff = pv0
    7-100: gg
